Attack Chain Explanation – Forum Points Manipulation & Account Lockout

This writeup is mainly about a Business Logic Vulnerability that led to points manipulation, leaderboard abuse, and finally account denial/lockout.

You can explain the attack chain step-by-step like this:

1. Recon & User Testing

The researcher explored the forum functionality manually.

He identified:

The forum had a ranking/points system
Users gained points for:
Liking posts
Solving issues
Sending badges
Forum activities

This phase is called:

Reconnaissance / Application Mapping

2. Initial Vulnerability Discovery – Point Farming

The attacker noticed:

Liking a post gave +5 points
When unliking the post:
The like count decreased
BUT the points were not removed

So the backend logic was flawed.

Vulnerability:

Improper business logic validation

Impact:
Infinite point generation
Leaderboard manipulation
3. Automation of the Attack

The attacker tried automating the like/unlike requests using:

Burp Suite Intruder
Macros
Repeated requests
Attack Flow:
Send Like request
Send Unlike request
Refresh profile page
Repeat automatically

Because the server kept awarding points every cycle:

Points increased continuously
No rate limiting existed
No duplicate action prevention existed
4. Expanding the Attack Surface – Notification Abuse

During testing, the attacker found:

Any user’s notifications could be viewed publicly

Initially, this looked like:

Broken Access Control (BAC)

But documentation showed this behavior was “intended.”

However:

Other users’ notifications still contained interactable actions
Those actions could also be liked/unliked
Result:

The attacker could farm even more points using:

Other users’ notifications
Badge notifications
5. Badge Abuse for Higher Rewards

The attacker discovered:

Badge notifications gave even more points
Each like/unlike cycle on badge activity generated around 30 points
Why this mattered:

The attacker found a higher-value object in the application.

This is:

Abuse of application reward mechanics

6. Full Exploitation Using Burp Macros

The attacker chained multiple requests together.

Requests Used:
Request	Purpose
Like request	Add points
Unlike request	Reset state
Profile/page request	Trigger refresh/update

Using Burp macros:

Requests executed repeatedly
Continuous point farming occurred automatically
7. Secondary Impact – Victim Account Lockout

After massive abuse:

Accounts started getting blocked
Victims saw a black screen / 400 Bad Request

The attacker realized:

They could intentionally trigger this on other users

Final Impact:
Permanent leaderboard manipulation
Account denial/lockout
Potential forum-wide abuse

This escalated the issue from:

“Informational”

to potentially:

Denial of Service (DoS) against user accounts

Root Cause Analysis

The application failed in several areas:

Weakness	Explanation
Business Logic Flaw	Points added repeatedly without validation
Missing State Validation	Unlike action didn’t reverse rewards
No Rate Limiting	Unlimited automated requests allowed
Poor Authorization Design	Public notification interactions exploitable
Lack of Abuse Detection	No anti-automation protection
Broken Reward System	Same action rewarded infinitely
Vulnerability Types Involved

You can mention these in your internship explanation:

Business Logic Vulnerability
Improper Authorization Design
Race Condition/Replay-style Abuse
Insufficient Rate Limiting
Denial of Service (Account Lockout)
Abuse of Gamification System
Simple Attack Chain Summary

You can explain it shortly like this:

Researcher tested forum features.
Found that liking posts gave points.
Unliking removed likes but not points.
Automated like/unlike requests using Burp Suite.
Discovered public notification interactions.
Abused badge notifications for higher point rewards.
Repeated requests massively increased points.
Excessive abuse corrupted victim account state.
Victims became locked out of the forum.
Real-World Security Lesson

This writeup shows that:

Even small logic flaws in reward systems can become serious security issues when chained together.

The issue was dangerous because it affected:

Integrity (fake leaderboard rankings)
Availability (users locked out)
Trust in the platform
